Subject: [PATCH] tb710fu: persist COM5 CDC across all kernel panics and halts

Every panic on this board terminates on the panicking CPU inside
panic_spin_forever() or panic_spin_shmcon(), never in halt_all_cpus():

  * boot-args debug=0x14e contains DB_NMI (0x4), and on arm64 debug.c:449
    turns DB_NMI into panicDebugging = TRUE, so debugger_collect_diagnostics()
    takes the `PE_i_can_has_debugger(NULL) && panicDebugging` branch (line
    1678), skips `if (!panicDebugging) kdp_machine_reboot_type(...)` (line
    1705) and ends in panic_spin_forever() (line 1710);
  * panic_spin_shmcon() is the branch before that, and this device has no
    astris/hardware shared-memory debugger, so its `for (;;) { wfe }` has
    nobody to talk to.

Two independent failures followed from that, both seen in pl44 (run
45553701071, COM5 log com5_pl44_gop.log):

  1. patch 0045's tb710fu_cdc_panic_loop() hook lives in halt_all_cpus(), so
     it is unreachable in this configuration and the panic loop never ran.
     The commit claim "enter tb710fu_cdc_panic_loop() before kPEPanicRestartCPU"
     was not implemented by this patch; only the nested-panic assert was.
  2. patch 0004 puts tb710fu_ramoops_panic_reset() first in
     panic_spin_forever() and in kdp_machine_reboot_type(); that flushes, waits
     ramoops_panic_delay (default 15 s) and issues PSCI SYSTEM_RESET. The
     console bytes of the panic were already tapped into the 64 KiB CDC ring
     (patch 0010 _cnputc/PE_kputc tap) but the ring is only transmitted by the
     DWC3 poll loop, so the reset ended the boot first: COM5 saw no panic
     byte at all and the port was gone afterwards (reopen returned 31).

This patch makes the panicking CPU hand the controller to the CDC shell
before any reset can fire, on both terminal paths and on the
kdp_machine_reboot_type() path used when panicDebugging is off:

  * panic_spin_forever(): drain the ring for 6 s, flush the ramoops zone (the
    panic still survives to Android whenever the operator resets later) and
    enter tb710fu_cdc_panic_loop(), which keeps polling DWC3 with interrupts
    off and executes the hands-free 'reboot' / 'edl' commands. The existing
    tb710fu_ramoops_panic_reset() stays as the no-CDC fallback.
  * panic_spin_shmcon(): same hand-over, after the existing
    PE_i_can_has_debugger()/handshake checks, so the astris spin can no longer
    park the CPU on a board without astris.
  * kdp_machine_reboot_type(): drain 4 s before tb710fu_ramoops_panic_reset(),
    so a reset that is going to happen cannot preempt the log either.
  * Remove the nested assert(panic_info) in PE_update_panicheader_nestedpanic()
    when gPanicBase == 0: there is no iBoot panic VRAM region on TB710FU, and
    the assert turned a nested panic into limit 5 and bypassed every hook.

Source: pristine XNU ac9718fb1af618d5ce8678d0dc6e8a58f252216f with patches
0001..0048 applied in series order; the pre-change source SHA-256 of each
edited file is in that patch's own header (0004 owns the debug.c
panic_spin_forever()/kdp_machine_reboot_type() hunk, 0010 owns the CDC tap,
0045 owns the halt_all_cpus() hook). pe_init.c and out_shmem.c are otherwise
untouched by this series, debug.c is touched by 0004 only.

Reason: a debug boot must deliver its own panic text over the channel that is
already open (COM5, dtr=1) and must keep the shell that can reset the device
hands-free. A reset before the drain destroys both.

Verification: git apply -v --check and a real apply pass for all four hunks on
the applied 0001..0048 fixture tree (.work/xnu-patch-lf-clean, XNU
ac9718f); the tree is then byte-identical to the reviewed new sources. The
drain provider is package/Tb710fuUsbCdc/CdcXnu.c tb710fu_cdc_panic_drain(),
which uses the same ownership proof (dma != NULL && probe_trace[1] != 0) and
the same DWC3 poll / rearm / pending-command step as the existing
tb710fu_cdc_panic_loop(), so a boot without an adopted controller falls back
to the previous reset behaviour instead of touching dead hardware. This is a
source and patch-application check; hardware panic drain, COM5 reopen and
hands-free 'reboot' still require a native run.

diff --git a/pexpert/arm/pe_init.c b/pexpert/arm/pe_init.c
index fd449f7..20377d9 100644
--- a/pexpert/arm/pe_init.c
+++ b/pexpert/arm/pe_init.c
@@ -737,8 +737,7 @@ PE_update_panicheader_nestedpanic()
 	 * if the panic header pointer is bogus (e.g. someone stomped on it) then bail.
 	 */
 	if (!panic_info) {
-		/* if this happens in development then blow up bigly */
-		assert(panic_info);
+		/* No iBoot panic VRAM region on TB710FU; avoid nested panic so the CDC panic loop stays reachable. */
 		return;
 	}
 
diff --git a/osfmk/kern/debug.c b/osfmk/kern/debug.c
index 6f2d4c1..b1e3a77 100644
--- a/osfmk/kern/debug.c
+++ b/osfmk/kern/debug.c
@@ -1371,6 +1371,27 @@ panic_spin_forever(void)
 void __attribute__((noinline, optnone))
 panic_spin_forever(void)
 {
+#if defined(XNU_TB710FU_CDC) && XNU_TB710FU_CDC
+	/*
+	 * debug=0x14e sets DB_NMI, which sets panicDebugging, so this function
+	 * (and panic_spin_shmcon()) is where every panic terminates and the
+	 * halt_all_cpus() hook of patch 0045 is never reached. Drain the console
+	 * bytes this CPU already tapped before anything can reset the SoC, then
+	 * stay in the shell: COM5 keeps the completion log and the hands-free
+	 * 'reboot' / 'edl' commands still work. Without an adopted controller
+	 * this returns 0 and the ramoops reset below is unchanged.
+	 */
+	{
+		extern int  tb710fu_cdc_panic_drain(unsigned milliseconds);
+		extern void tb710fu_ramoops_flush(void);
+		extern void tb710fu_cdc_panic_loop(void);
+
+		if (tb710fu_cdc_panic_drain(6000u)) {
+			tb710fu_ramoops_flush();
+			tb710fu_cdc_panic_loop();
+		}
+	}
+#endif
 	/* TB710FU: flush the zone and reset, so the panic survives to Android. */
 	tb710fu_ramoops_panic_reset();
 	for (;;) {
@@ -1393,7 +1414,18 @@ kdp_machine_reboot_type(unsigned int type, uint64_t debugger_flags)
 static void
 kdp_machine_reboot_type(unsigned int type, uint64_t debugger_flags)
 {
 	if (type == kPEPanicRestartCPU) {
+#if defined(XNU_TB710FU_CDC) && XNU_TB710FU_CDC
+		/*
+		 * The reset below is what makes COM5 disappear. Give the bytes
+		 * already sitting in the CDC ring to the host first.
+		 */
+		{
+			extern int tb710fu_cdc_panic_drain(unsigned milliseconds);
+
+			(void)tb710fu_cdc_panic_drain(4000u);
+		}
+#endif
 		tb710fu_ramoops_panic_reset();
 	}
 	if ((type == kPEPanicRestartCPU) && (debugger_flags & DEBUGGER_OPTION_SKIP_PANICEND_CALLOUTS)) {
diff --git a/osfmk/kdp/output_stages/out_shmem.c b/osfmk/kdp/output_stages/out_shmem.c
index 9c2a5b0..e0d9f12 100644
--- a/osfmk/kdp/output_stages/out_shmem.c
+++ b/osfmk/kdp/output_stages/out_shmem.c
@@ -657,8 +657,28 @@ panic_spin_shmcon(void)
 	if (hwsd_info == NULL) {
 		kern_coredump_log(NULL, "handshake structure not initialized\n");
 		return;
 	}
 
+#if defined(XNU_TB710FU_CDC) && XNU_TB710FU_CDC
+	/*
+	 * This device has no astris and no hardware shared-memory debugger, so
+	 * the for (;;) below would park the panicking CPU with no peer and COM5
+	 * would never receive the panic text. Drain, persist and hand the CPU to
+	 * the CDC shell instead; the astris handshake is left untouched for any
+	 * boot that does have one.
+	 */
+	{
+		extern int  tb710fu_cdc_panic_drain(unsigned milliseconds);
+		extern void tb710fu_ramoops_flush(void);
+		extern void tb710fu_cdc_panic_loop(void);
+
+		if (tb710fu_cdc_panic_drain(6000u)) {
+			tb710fu_ramoops_flush();
+			tb710fu_cdc_panic_loop();
+		}
+	}
+#endif
+
 	kern_coredump_log(NULL, "\nPlease go to https://panic.apple.com to report this panic\n");
 	kern_coredump_log(NULL, "Waiting for hardware shared memory debugger, handshake structure is at virt: %p, phys %p\n",
 	    hwsd_info, (void *)kvtophys((vm_offset_t)hwsd_info));
